# Darwin self-evolution log

One line per change. Columns: date / commit / src / action / target / one-line reason.
src: user = explicitly commanded by the user (high weight, agent MUST NOT revert); auto = autonomous agent judgment.
The full account lives in that commit's body. Read it with `git show <hash>`, not by default.
Actions: new | adapt (fit to this machine) | fix | revert | scope (narrow) | retract (paragraph-level)

2026-09-01  a1b2c3d  auto  new     cards/zsh-log.md +1     zsh builtin `log` shadows /usr/bin/log; note plus index line
2026-09-02  d4e5f6a  auto  adapt   skills/deploy/SKILL.md  upstream assumes GNU coreutils, this host ships BSD versions
2026-09-03  b7c8d9e  auto  fix     cards/zsh-log.md        root cause said "permissions tightened", actually shell builtin shadowing
2026-09-03  c9d0e1f  auto  revert  skills/deploy/SKILL.md  undoes d4e5f6a: the BSD failure came from a stale PATH, not from coreutils
2026-09-04  e2f3a4b  auto  scope   cards/driver-cli.md     the empty-tree finding applies to Safari only; Chrome untested
2026-09-06  f8a12bc  user  revert  skills/deploy/SKILL.md  user explicitly directed removing deployment hook; agent cannot override

## Reading this log

The `src` column explicitly records the provenance and authority behind each evolution:
- `user`: Initiated by direct user instruction ("remember this", "fix this card", "never run X"). High precedence. Future agents are strictly prohibited from reverting `user` entries autonomously.
- `auto`: Autonomous hypothesis generated by an agent during routine reflection. Subsequent agents may independently audit, scope, or revert `auto` entries when contradictory runtime evidence emerges.

The `revert` entry on 2026-09-03 exemplifies the format's design. It records not just that the `adapt` was undone, but why its underlying evidence was flawed. Without that second half, the next agent encountering the same BSD-looking failure would reach the identical false conclusion and repeat the bad edit.

Counting `revert` and `fix` lines per file is the thrash check:

```bash
grep -cE "  (revert|fix)  <file> " DARWIN.md
```

Two reverts on one file, or three fixes while the problem still persists, indicates that the root cause remains unidentified. In this event, editing must pause and escalate to a human operator. `adapt` entries do not count toward thrashing, as adjusting upstream skills to local environment constraints is routine and expected.
